  "result": "Implemented `wire/codec.py`. The full suite passes:\n\n```\n6 passed in 0.02s\n```\n\n**Approach chosen:** A, compact binary. The schema is fixed and purely numeric, so packing each record with `struct` as `>IHdB` gives bit-exact float round trips for the awkward cases the tests probe (`-0.0`, NaN, `1e300`) without any JSON special-casing, and a fixed layout makes truncation and corruption detection precise.\n\n**Format details:**\n- Header is a 4-byte magic `WIRE`, a version byte, and a uint32 record count.\n- Each record is 15 bytes at a computable offset, big-endian.\n- A trailing CRC32 covers the header and body.\n\n**Validation in `decode`:** it rejects bad magic, unknown version, any length that disagrees with the declared count, and checksum mismatches, all raising `CodecError`. `encode` also raises `CodecError` for records with missing or extra fields, wrong types, or out-of-range integers. I spot-checked appended bytes, single-bit corruption, empty input, and `inf` beyond the shipped tests, and all behave correctly.\n\nThe only new tracked file is `wire/codec.py`. I did not commit, since you didn't ask for one.",
